On a calm morning off the coast of a small island, Dr. Leah Okonkwo lowers a hydrophone into the water and puts on her headphones. To an untrained ear, the sound is a continuous crackle, like bacon frying in a distant kitchen. To her, it is a medical report. “Healthy reef,” she says after a minute. “Lots of shrimp, and some parrotfish feeding.” She has been listening to reefs for fifteen years, and she can now tell within seconds whether a stretch of coral is thriving or dying. It is a skill she never planned to acquire. Her original ambition, as a student, was to become a conventional marine biologist who spent her days counting fish, and she assumed that a life with a notebook and a boat would be quiet enough to suit her.

{{1}}

What she had stumbled on was, she later realized, an entirely unmapped territory. Biologists had long known that reefs were noisy, but the noise had been treated as a curiosity rather than as a measurement. Okonkwo's recordings suggested that it could be used as one: a reef bleached by warm water, she found, falls quiet within a season, long before the damage shows up clearly to a diver's eye. Nobody, as far as she could discover, had ever compared the sound of a living reef with that of a dying one in any systematic way.

{{2}}

That speed transformed the argument. Within three years her team had recorders on reefs in four countries, and conservation groups were using the results to decide where to spend their limited funds. But the project that brought her wider attention was a stranger one. Having shown that dead reefs fall silent, she began to wonder whether the silence might itself be part of the problem, and she spent several months reading everything she could find about how young fish behave in open water.

{{3}}

To test the idea, her team placed underwater speakers on patches of dead coral and played recordings of a thriving reef for six weeks. Nearby they left similar patches in silence. The results were more dramatic than anyone had predicted: the patches with sound attracted far more fish, and a wider variety of species. Okonkwo remains careful about the claim. Sound will not bring a reef back to life on its own, she stresses, since the corals themselves still have to survive, and no recording can protect them from the heat.

{{4}}

Those precautions reflect a temperament that colleagues describe as cautious to the point of stubbornness. She will not publish a result until it has been reproduced in at least two locations, and she keeps a notebook of the experiments that failed, which she calls her “museum of mistakes”. Younger researchers in her laboratory are encouraged to add to it, and anyone who works with her for long learns that “probably” is a word she distrusts. “If you only record your successes,” she tells them, “you learn nothing about how you might be wrong.”

{{5}}

That reaction is what keeps her going when the data are discouraging, and lately they often have been. Marine heat waves have struck several of her study sites, and two of the quietest reefs she has recorded were, only a few years ago, among the loudest. She admits that there are days when listening feels like attending a funeral. “But a doctor who stops reading the chart because the news is bad,” she says, “is no use to anyone.”

{{6}}

It is a small result, and she is the first to say so. Yet as the boat turns back toward the harbor and she lifts the hydrophone out of the water, she looks content. By evening, the software will have turned the morning's crackle into a single number on a map. “A reef that can be heard is a reef that can still be saved,” she says. “That is all I am asking people to do: listen before it is too late.”
